## Why This Is Asked
Advanced GraphQL testing in multi-tenant SaaS is complex due to caching, ACLs, and nested resolvers. This question probes the ability to design end-to-end tests that catch N+1, improper isolation, and stale caches during deployments.

## Key Concepts
- GraphQL persisted queries and hash-based routing
- DataLoader-like batching to prevent N+1
- Depth/complexity controls and per-tenant ACLs
- Cache invalidation across tenants and schema rollouts

## Code Example
```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check } from 'k6';
export const options = { vus: 100, duration: '30s' };
export default function () {
  const body = JSON.stringify({
    extensions: {"persistedQuery": {"version": 1, "sha256Hash": "abcd1234"}},
    variables: {"tenantId": "t1", "id": "u123"}
  });
  const res = http.post('https://api.example.com/graphql', body, {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
  });
  check(res, { 'status is 200': (r) => r.status === 200 });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate canary schema rollouts to minimize blast radius?
- What metrics and thresholds would you assert for N+1 detection across tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical end-to-end reliability under high concurrency, including idempotency, external dependencies, and reconciliation. 

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys, Redis-backed deduplication
- Asynchronous queues, external bank API faults, retries
- Webhook delivery guarantees and replay protection
- Ledger reconciliation and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('../src/app');

test('idempotent payments with same key yields single ledger write', async () => {
  const key = 'abc123';
  const payload = { amount: 100, currency: 'USD', merchant: 'ACME' };
  const t1 = request(app).post('/payments').set('Idempotency-Key', key).send(payload);
  const t2 = request(app).post('/payments').set('Idempotency-Key', key).send(payload);
  const [r1, r2] = await Promise.all([t1, t2]);
  expect(r1.status).toBe(200);
  expect(r2.status).toBe(200);
  // verify a single ledger entry created via test hooks or API mocks
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test idempotency-key expiry and cleanup? 
- How would you simulate downstream bank outages and ensure no double-charges occur?

Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical API-testing with event-driven, multi-tenant systems and guarantees like at-least-once delivery, idempotency, and DLQ routing.

Key Concepts
- Idempotency per tenant
- End-to-end delivery guarantees
- Failure modes: broker outage, consumer downtime
- Observability: audit logs and DLQs

Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test: ensure duplicate webhook does not double-process
async function testIdempotentWebhook(httpPost, tenantId, event){
  const first = await httpPost(`/tenants/${tenantId}/webhooks`, event)
  const dup = await httpPost(`/tenants/${tenantId}/webhooks`, event)
  assert(first.status === 200 && dup.status === 200)
  // verify idempotent side-effects only occurred once
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate replay tests against a canary environment?
- How would you verify DLQ routing correctness under mixed failure modes?

